  "replies": [
   "I've built an nf-core/methylseq run for your 2 samples. It hasn't been submitted yet.\n\n**What the run gives you:** a methylation level for every CpG site the reads cover, in each sample.\n\n**What it doesn't do:** it won't report methylation per promoter. To get that, the per-CpG output has to be averaged over promoter regions afterwards, for example a window around each gene's start taken from the GENCODE v46 gene annotation. Neither the pipeline nor NExtSEEK does that step.\n\n**Samples:** one samplesheet with 2 samples, both human HCT116 colon cells sequenced with RRBS (single-end, 75 bp).\n- D.SEQ-261110NA-13 (NTC-METH-1): treated with DMSO\n- D.SEQ-261110NA-14 (NTC-METH-2): treated with 1 \u00b5M 5-azacytidine\n\nSince there is one sample per treatment, you can see the raw difference in promoter methylation between the two. Testing whether that difference is real needs at least two, ideally three, replicates per group.\n\n**Run settings:**\n- **Pipeline:** nf-core/methylseq\n- **Aligner:** bismark\n- **RRBS mode:** on, to suit the MspI-digested libraries; this also skips removing duplicate reads\n- **Cytosine report:** on, giving the methylation call at each cytosine, separately for each DNA strand\n- **Reference:** the local Luria GRCh38 genome (GRCh38.primary_assembly.genome.fa.gz). The bisulfite version of the genome index will be built during the run and saved.\n- **Parameter check:** the settings passed the pipeline's own check.\n- **Run time:** there's no estimate for this pipeline yet.\n\n**One thing to check:** the libraries were made with the Ovation RRBS Methyl-Seq kit, which usually needs its own adapter-trimming step. I can't set that from here, so the trimming will be the standard RRBS kind.\n\nThe run is built and ready. Tell me if you'd like to change anything, such as using bwameth as the aligner or turning the cytosine report off. Once it finishes, just tell Nessie \"my run is done\" and Nessie will turn the outputs into NExtSEEK upload workbooks for you to review."
  ],
